Day 32 – Docker Volumes & Networking

Objective

Learn how Docker handles data persistence and container communication using Volumes and Networks.



Task 1 – Data Loss Without Volume

Steps Performed

- Pulled MySQL image
- Started a MySQL container
- Created a database and inserted data
- Stopped and removed the container
- Started a new MySQL container

Observation

The database and data were lost after deleting the container.

Reason

Containers are ephemeral. Without a volume, all data is stored inside the container and is deleted when the container is removed.



Task 2 – Named Volumes

Steps Performed

- Created a named volume ("mysql-data")
- Attached the volume to a MySQL container
- Created a database and inserted data
- Removed the container
- Started a new container using the same volume

Verification

- "docker volume ls"
- "docker volume inspect mysql-data"

Observation

The database and data were still available after creating a new container.

Conclusion

Named Volumes provide persistent storage and are ideal for databases.



Task 3 – Bind Mounts

Steps Performed

- Created a local folder with an "index.html" file
- Mounted it into an Nginx container
- Opened the webpage in a browser
- Edited "index.html" on the host
- Refreshed the browser

Observation

The webpage updated instantly without restarting the container.

Difference

Named Volume| Bind Mount
Managed by Docker| Managed by User
Best for Databases| Best for Source Code & Config Files
Stored in Docker| Stored on Host Machine



Task 4 – Docker Networking Basics

Commands Used

- "docker network ls"
- "docker network inspect bridge"

Observation

- Containers on the default bridge network could communicate using IP Address.
- They could not communicate using container names.

Reason

The default bridge network does not provide automatic DNS-based name resolution.



Task 5 – Custom Bridge Network

Steps Performed

- Created a custom bridge network ("my-app-net")
- Started two containers on the same network
- Verified communication using container names

Observation

Containers successfully communicated using their names.

Reason

Docker provides built-in DNS on custom bridge networks.



Task 6 – Final Project

Created:

- Custom Network ("my-app-net")
- Named Volume ("mysql-data")
- MySQL Database Container
- App Container

Verified

- Data persistence using the volume
- Container-to-container communication using container names



Key Learnings

- Containers are temporary.
- Volumes keep data safe.
- Named Volumes are best for databases.
- Bind Mounts are useful for development.
- Default Bridge supports IP communication.
- Custom Bridge supports name-based communication.
- Docker's internal DNS works on custom bridge networks.



